Do colour meanings differ between cultures
title end

section start
title start
Abstract
title end
paragraph start
Question: do colour meanings differ between cultures. Method: a secondary article on a study across 55 countries and a popular summary. Result: associations rooted in nature tend to be shared across populations, while culturally built ones differ. Limit: the primary paper was not read and the audience location is not known.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Introduction
title end
paragraph start
The audience may be international, so this page asks whether colour meanings travel.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Methods
title end
paragraph start
Read secondary sources only.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Results
title end
paragraph start
A study across 55 countries, reported through a secondary article, found that colour associations tied to natural referents tend to be shared, while culturally constructed ones vary (Jonauskaite and co-authors, 2020).
paragraph end
paragraph start
A 2013 study summarised by Fast Company reported that an effect of red on attraction appeared beyond Western samples.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Discussion
title end
paragraph start
Until the audience location is known, avoid colour meanings that depend on culture and rely on shared ones. This is a judgement, not a finding.
paragraph end
paragraph start
todo: read the primary paper and fill in the audience location
Needs the audience research.
paragraph end
